<p>North Korean leader Kim Jong-un told parliament in Pyongyang that his government would “irreversibly” strengthen its status as a nuclear-armed state, keep expanding its nuclear forces, and treat South Korea as the country’s “most hostile” state. The New York Times reported that Kim tied that message to the Iran war, arguing that North Korea’s nuclear arsenal shields it from what he described as U.S. hostility and pressure. The speech reinforced Pyongyang’s deterrence narrative at a moment of broader international instability and signaled that progress on denuclearization or inter-Korean engagement is unlikely in the near term.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Pfkij1rDYtIZ6A8MthWL3DWM8sMpWPhTT7nS8tB-P2zZkJQdNzuphk9u2yIs14NhHP9jnUrupQQMSyCxAuK-k1myaMrarp1sSnJDkjuciigIuJWiRmTNHJKY9Hl6A-Vv2ns9y64CwHnO3fJrOC-anLMTEjoCoeD6St3vSGtg-qlO5Fedz3qfgj7lhmfIg71ClDLa2-H7' alt='A photo released by North Korean state media showing the country’s leader, Kim Jong-un, speaking at Parliament in Pyongyang on Monday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Trump omission: SCMP reported that Kim accused the United States of “state terror” but did not directly criticize President Trump, which analysts said appeared calibrated to preserve at least a narrow diplomatic channel with Washington.</li><li>Seoul downgraded: Analysts cited by The Hindu said Kim’s vilification of South Korea reflects his view that Seoul is no longer a useful intermediary with Washington, unlike during the 2018-2019 Trump-Kim summit period.</li><li>Policy permanence: DW said Kim told parliament he would “firmly consolidate” North Korea’s nuclear-armed status, underscoring that Pyongyang now casts its weapons program as a permanent part of state policy rather than a temporary bargaining chip.</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/23/world/asia/north-korea-kim-iran-nuclear.html'>How North Korea’s Kim Jong-un Is Using the Iran War to Justify His Nuclear Arsenal</a> - nytimes.com